# `only` before a verb can limit the verb or anything after it (`the
# hook only fails on growth` versus `the hook fails only on growth`), so
# the reader has to pick. The technical-writing skill keeps `only`
# beside the word it limits. The rule counts `only` before a verb when a
# subject sits right before it (`the hook only fails`). It also counts
# `only` after an auxiliary, a helping verb such as `is`, `has`, or
# `can` (`can only run`, `has only seen`). The filter narrows the
# finding to `only` and the verb.
#
# A progressive, a form of `be` plus an `-ing` verb (`is only running`),
# stays out, because the tagger reads it as part of the verb phrase.
# `need` stays out, because the obligation rule reports `needs to`.
# `that`, `which`, and `who` before `only` stay out. In `requests that
# only read objects`, `only` limits the requests to the ones that read,
# so moving it changes the meaning.
#
# It hit 339 lines in the measure.py corpus, and in 20 of 20 sampled
# hits `only` can move beside the word it limits (`can only run on
# Linux` becomes `can run only on Linux`). Some writers mean the verb
# itself (`references only borrow`), and the rule misfires there.
expr verb <VERB, !PROG, ![need, needs]>
expr lead <[NOUN, PRON, PROPN, can, cannot, must, should, will, would, could, may, might, is, are, was, were, be, been, has, have, had, do, does, did], ![that, which, who]>
expr main <(@lead only @verb), (only @verb)>
let message "Misplaced `only`. Move `only` beside the word it limits."
let description "Flags `only` between a subject or a helping verb and the verb after it, where it can limit the verb or anything after it."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The hook fails only on growth."
allows "Only the owner can delete the file."
allows "You can set the flag only once."
allows "The hook is only running once."
allows "The webhook skips requests that only read objects."
